WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.480
How Wall Street hides big trades.

2
00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:09.360
Nick Bostrom on controlling superintelligence and why startup geography still matters.

3
00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:17.920
Plus, Raul Powell's case for investing through technological disruption, not freezing in fear.

4
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:28.560
On complex systems with Patrick McKenzie, Alex Ellie explains how algorithms slice and route million share orders without revealing the full trade.

5
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:35.360
Because once rivals detect that pattern, they can move the price against you.

6
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.960
Like someone can see that a buyer sell happened.

7
00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:48.320
But they don't want to tip their hand as to like, okay, if I'm selling a million shares on Microsoft, it's quite likely that that is not the only Microsoft I will be selling that day.

8
00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:58.880
And so that uh might suggest to the rest of the market, like, hey, the price of Microsoft is probably going down unless someone really, really wants Microsoft right now.

9
00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:17.680
And uh this is one of the the sort of core problems in trading is doing large block trades in a in ways such that you get the execution you want at a price that is acceptable without informing the rest of the market that you are doing a large block trade because that will have what is typically called price impact.

10
00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:24.800
Uh the rest of the market will understand the totality of the trade before you put it on and uh start quoting less for your rule numbers to you.

11
00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.600
Yes, yeah, and and you bring up at an excellent point, and I I can talk about some different ways that people do it.

12
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:38.960
So let's say you are a uh a firm and you have some alpha or or signal or you have some state on where you think things are going to go.

13
00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:45.440
One thing you could do is you could tell, let's say you want to go through a broker, you could tell the broker, hey, here's this giant order, work it for me.

14
00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:48.160
Now the broker knows that you think this is the case.

15
00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:52.800
And so there are rules to prevent them taking advantage of that, aka front running.

16
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:57.040
But if you just put out this giant block, as you mentioned, other people will see it.

17
00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:01.160
So some of the techniques you can do is you can uh chop up the order over the day.

18
00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:09.880
And there's different ways you can do that in terms of uh what's called smart routing, where you give it to AK an algorithm and we'll make lots of little pieces and it'll send it to different places.

19
00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:12.280
You can send it to different venues yourself.

20
00:02:12.280 --> 00:02:18.360
So you might have five or six different brokers and you can say I'm gonna give 10% to this broker, 20% to this broker.

21
00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:29.480
You might even go to the investment bank and they you can say something like, Hey, I want you to execute this million for me so it won't show up as me executing it and it'll show up as you.

22
00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:36.120
And this gets more into what's called reg and a mess and how orders are routed and fills are sent to clients, et cetera.

23
00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:43.560
But uh the the bank can then chop it up themselves, they can give you one big fill, and then they can aka work the order over the day.

24
00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:49.320
So yeah, there's a lot of different kind of flows through the system to basically uh to get you the position that you want.

25
00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:52.200
Um, some firms do it over multiple days, et cetera.

26
00:02:52.280 --> 00:03:36.280
And for each of these, there are people with uh generally opposed uh incentives, where uh you know if you are aware that there are technologies to like chop an order up over the course of a working day, then that suggests that uh at 11.02 in the morning, you might be able to say, wait a minute, you know, if the naive way to do this, and believe me, people have tried the naive way to do this, if the naive way to do this is just like sending in an order every five minutes, and we've seen dozens of orders at this point that are all for the same quantity and the same in the same symbol at the same price, then I naturally assume that I will see more dozens of orders through the rest of the day and can start positioning myself uh uh against those orders before they have actually been uh made.

27
00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:37.400
Yes, that's that's correct.

28
00:03:37.400 --> 00:03:52.480
Yeah, they um without going into too many of the alpha details, firms look at all kinds of data on just smart execution is partly a contest over who can infer the other side's next move first.

29
00:03:52.560 --> 00:04:05.040
On odd lots, philosopher Nick Bostrom revisits his path from the 2014 book Superintelligence to today's central problem controlling systems more capable than their creators.

30
00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:10.560
That question feels less theoretical as frontier models keep gaining autonomy.

31
00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:23.280
And then you also have superintelligence, which seemed to paint a different scenario where AI poses a physical and possibly existential danger to humanity.

32
00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:27.040
Which one do you think we're closer to right now?

33
00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.120
We are somewhere in the middle, maybe.

34
00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.160
Uh so the first one was superintelligence.

35
00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:37.360
It came out in 2014, and I had been working on it for uh six years before that.

36
00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:49.040
And at that time, the whole notion of having human level or or even super intelligent machines was generally just ignored, or you know, people dismissed it as science fiction.

37
00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:56.960
And it seemed to me clear that this would one day happen if science and technology were allowed to continue on a wide front.

38
00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:02.400
We would eventually figure out how to make smart algorithms and build big computers.

39
00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:21.120
And that once we succeeded with that, we would then be faced with uh kind of a bunch of challenges, including this alignment problem of how how you can sort of design a mind more brilliant than any human and still steer it so that it will actually have an impact on the world that is beneficial.

40
00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:28.480
So that I guess, like with many other developments, helped eventually build a kind of global conversation.

41
00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:34.440
And so now all the frontier labs have research teams trying to develop scalable methods for AI control.

42
00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:43.160
And um the conversation really has kind of swung, and there's like this rise of people really worried about what will happen.

43
00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:47.320
And I thought it might be time to have a look at the other side of the coin.

44
00:05:47.320 --> 00:05:51.960
Like what happens if things actually go well, if if we solve this alignment problem.

45
00:05:51.960 --> 00:06:03.480
Let's also imagine we solve to the extent, whatever extent it can be solved, like the governance challenge, like we don't use this technology to oppress each other or wage war, and we get like the best possible outcome.

46
00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:20.920
Like what would a human life actually look like in this kind of I call it a solved world, where all practical problems either have already been solved or to the extent that they remain, or are better worked on by these uh superintelligent machines and robots, and where there is no longer any need for human economic labor.

47
00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:34.120
And in fact, if you think more deeply about it, you realize it's not just earning wages that becomes unnecessary for humans to do, but but a lot of other instrumentally motivated efforts as well would be a good idea.

48
00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:41.560
Capability and control are separate engineering problems, and progress on one does not guarantee the other.

49
00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:03.080
Yeah.

